1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,428
Twenty-six billion dollars.

2
00:00:01,753 --> 00:00:02,798
That's what Nvidia,

3
00:00:02,914 --> 00:00:05,828
Microsoft, Google and four other suppliers just poured

4
00:00:05,863 --> 00:00:07,430
into Anthropic's IPO.

5
00:00:08,127 --> 00:00:09,880
Ed Zitron calls it the last money out

6
00:00:09,915 --> 00:00:11,981
the door. Because if he's right,

7
00:00:12,132 --> 00:00:14,559
the next AI company that needs cash may

8
00:00:14,629 --> 00:00:15,964
find nobody left to pay.

9
00:00:16,289 --> 00:00:17,357
In the next ten minutes:

10
00:00:17,439 --> 00:00:19,447
the debut, the missing filing,

11
00:00:19,656 --> 00:00:21,107
and the companies he has put on death

12
00:00:21,165 --> 00:00:21,781
watch.

13
00:00:21,833 --> 00:00:23,737
Part one. The debut.

14
00:00:24,225 --> 00:00:26,628
How did the year's biggest AI listing go?

15
00:00:27,057 --> 00:00:29,310
Anthropic, the maker of Claude,

16
00:00:29,577 --> 00:00:31,818
listed at $83 a share.

17
00:00:32,177 --> 00:00:33,571
As Zitron recorded,

18
00:00:33,617 --> 00:00:34,767
it traded higher,

19
00:00:34,929 --> 00:00:37,181
but struggled to stay above $100.

20
00:00:37,541 --> 00:00:40,943
His verdict: not the massive return people expected.

21
00:00:41,373 --> 00:00:43,242
But first days say little.

22
00:00:43,671 --> 00:00:45,343
So let's look at the business.

23
00:00:45,610 --> 00:00:46,806
First, growth.

24
00:00:47,584 --> 00:00:49,546
Citing spending data from Ramp,

25
00:00:49,813 --> 00:00:52,507
he says Anthropic's growth this quarter is trending

26
00:00:52,576 --> 00:00:54,689
toward 15 to 25%.

27
00:00:55,119 --> 00:00:56,071
A quarter ago,

28
00:00:56,117 --> 00:00:57,708
it was 47%.

29
00:00:57,975 --> 00:00:59,461
A few quarters before that,

30
00:00:59,508 --> 00:01:01,145
around 150%.

31
00:01:01,563 --> 00:01:03,304
That's not a rocket anymore.

32
00:01:03,571 --> 00:01:05,487
That's a rocket running out of fuel.

33
00:01:06,242 --> 00:01:06,892
Now the bills.

34
00:01:06,927 --> 00:01:11,211
Anthropic has signed up for $895 billion of

35
00:01:11,292 --> 00:01:15,100
commitments, mostly computing power that hasn't even started.

36
00:01:15,448 --> 00:01:17,759
And $435 billion

37
00:01:18,119 --> 00:01:19,361
of that, he says,

38
00:01:19,442 --> 00:01:20,568
can't be cancelled.

39
00:01:21,056 --> 00:01:22,658
Here's the number that really matters.

40
00:01:23,227 --> 00:01:26,826
The Financial Times' Robert Armstrong calculated that for

41
00:01:26,873 --> 00:01:28,103
a decent return,

42
00:01:28,336 --> 00:01:31,192
Anthropic would need about $600 billion of revenue

43
00:01:31,215 --> 00:01:32,654
a year by 2030.

44
00:01:33,432 --> 00:01:36,950
Microsoft, with monopolies across office software,

45
00:01:37,298 --> 00:01:40,421
made about $331 billion last year.

46
00:01:40,758 --> 00:01:43,057
Anthropic would need nearly twice that.

47
00:01:43,394 --> 00:01:44,183
To be fair,

48
00:01:44,311 --> 00:01:45,251
there's a cushion:

49
00:01:45,669 --> 00:01:48,897
over $200 billion in cash after the IPO,

50
00:01:49,198 --> 00:01:50,313
by Zitron's count.

51
00:01:50,847 --> 00:01:52,704
But big prepayments are coming,

52
00:01:52,716 --> 00:01:56,234
and he says anything under $21 billion of

53
00:01:56,315 --> 00:01:58,138
revenue this quarter would be a problem.

54
00:01:58,625 --> 00:02:00,297
Even 15% growth,

55
00:02:00,320 --> 00:02:01,238
in his math,

56
00:02:01,307 --> 00:02:02,224
isn't enough.

57
00:02:02,654 --> 00:02:04,395
Which raises the obvious question.

58
00:02:04,883 --> 00:02:06,230
Who was buying these shares,

59
00:02:06,265 --> 00:02:07,252
and why?

60
00:02:07,300 --> 00:02:09,854
Part two. The last money out the door.

61
00:02:10,202 --> 00:02:12,002
Look at who wrote the biggest checks.

62
00:02:12,048 --> 00:02:14,951
Nvidia. Microsoft. Google.

63
00:02:15,206 --> 00:02:18,306
AMD. TSMC. Broadcom.

64
00:02:18,422 --> 00:02:19,247
Micron.

65
00:02:19,665 --> 00:02:23,252
Together, about $26 billion into the IPO.

66
00:02:24,007 --> 00:02:24,761
Notice something?

67
00:02:25,179 --> 00:02:27,107
These are Anthropic's suppliers.

68
00:02:27,176 --> 00:02:28,396
In Zitron's view,

69
00:02:28,558 --> 00:02:29,824
they were topping up a customer,

70
00:02:29,847 --> 00:02:31,182
so it can keep paying them,

71
00:02:31,414 --> 00:02:33,609
and so early investors could cash out.

72
00:02:34,282 --> 00:02:35,965
That's the last money out the door.

73
00:02:36,325 --> 00:02:37,869
Retail buyers near the top,

74
00:02:37,904 --> 00:02:40,482
he notes, lost money almost immediately.

75
00:02:41,155 --> 00:02:43,373
Here's why that matters for everyone else.

76
00:02:43,466 --> 00:02:45,590
Next in line is OpenAI.

77
00:02:45,950 --> 00:02:47,576
It just raised $11 billion,

78
00:02:47,889 --> 00:02:51,581
at a $1.25 trillion valuation.

79
00:02:52,069 --> 00:02:54,344
Reports say it wanted $30 billion,

80
00:02:54,704 --> 00:02:56,620
at $1.4 trillion.

81
00:02:57,398 --> 00:03:00,161
Oddly, one Bloomberg report stressed the money had

82
00:03:00,196 --> 00:03:01,798
actually been wired and received.

83
00:03:02,227 --> 00:03:03,690
Zitron had never seen that.

84
00:03:04,050 --> 00:03:05,478
And OpenAI's numbers,

85
00:03:05,559 --> 00:03:06,628
as he cites them,

86
00:03:06,662 --> 00:03:10,134
are rough. A loss of $10.9 billion in

87
00:03:10,227 --> 00:03:13,582
one quarter, on about $10 billion of revenue.

88
00:03:14,151 --> 00:03:15,695
Yes, margins improved:

89
00:03:15,962 --> 00:03:19,933
from about minus 150% to minus 100%.

90
00:03:20,502 --> 00:03:23,451
And its newest model got a cold reception,

91
00:03:23,718 --> 00:03:25,227
even from its cheerleaders.

92
00:03:25,657 --> 00:03:28,176
He expects OpenAI to need more money early

93
00:03:28,246 --> 00:03:28,908
next year.

94
00:03:29,477 --> 00:03:30,881
But its biggest backers,

95
00:03:30,916 --> 00:03:33,064
he says, chipped in only a few hundred

96
00:03:33,099 --> 00:03:35,781
million, because they're short on cash too.

97
00:03:36,558 --> 00:03:38,137
So his question is blunt.

98
00:03:38,567 --> 00:03:41,249
After Anthropic soaked up this much money,

99
00:03:41,411 --> 00:03:43,373
who's left to fund OpenAI?

100
00:03:43,687 --> 00:03:45,893
And the biggest supplier of all just went

101
00:03:45,974 --> 00:03:47,843
quiet on something important.

102
00:03:47,900 --> 00:03:49,920
Part three. The missing filing.

103
00:03:50,489 --> 00:03:52,695
Nvidia reported earnings this week,

104
00:03:52,799 --> 00:03:55,621
under interim chief executive Colette Kress,

105
00:03:55,981 --> 00:03:57,467
the former finance chief.

106
00:03:57,827 --> 00:03:58,848
On the surface:

107
00:03:59,278 --> 00:04:00,311
record numbers,

108
00:04:00,671 --> 00:04:03,620
beat expectations, raised forecast.

109
00:04:04,049 --> 00:04:06,476
But Zitron spotted what wasn't there.

110
00:04:07,045 --> 00:04:09,936
Normally, Nvidia files its quarterly report,

111
00:04:10,017 --> 00:04:12,746
the ten-Q, alongside earnings.

112
00:04:13,013 --> 00:04:14,115
This time, nothing.

113
00:04:14,789 --> 00:04:16,205
The deadline, he says,

114
00:04:16,228 --> 00:04:17,540
is December 4th,

115
00:04:17,773 --> 00:04:20,443
and Kress promised it within the coming weeks.

116
00:04:21,116 --> 00:04:23,519
Bulls say it's just extra checks during the

117
00:04:23,566 --> 00:04:25,714
investigation into Jensen Huang.

118
00:04:26,282 --> 00:04:27,757
Zitron isn't convinced:

119
00:04:27,838 --> 00:04:30,416
the scandal broke just a week before earnings,

120
00:04:30,776 --> 00:04:33,086
so the filing was likely nearly done.

121
00:04:33,574 --> 00:04:36,430
His words: we need answers from Nvidia.

122
00:04:37,207 --> 00:04:38,148
On the scandal,

123
00:04:38,183 --> 00:04:39,808
he cites the Wall Street Journal,

124
00:04:39,970 --> 00:04:42,339
which he says now puts chip sales through

125
00:04:42,374 --> 00:04:44,092
Megaspeed at around $12 billion.

126
00:04:44,661 --> 00:04:46,426
Those are reports and allegations,

127
00:04:46,460 --> 00:04:47,331
not findings.

128
00:04:47,900 --> 00:04:48,690
Then his guest,

129
00:04:48,794 --> 00:04:51,673
Steve from the hardware channel Gamers Nexus,

130
00:04:51,836 --> 00:04:53,554
pulls up Nvidia's own numbers.

131
00:04:54,460 --> 00:04:57,815
Revenue booked to Singapore went from about $760

132
00:04:57,896 --> 00:04:59,649
million in one quarter

133
00:05:00,114 --> 00:05:02,505
to about $4 billion a year later.

134
00:05:03,156 --> 00:05:04,630
Over one fiscal year,

135
00:05:04,851 --> 00:05:06,290
roughly $24 billion.

136
00:05:06,940 --> 00:05:08,984
And then it vanished from the breakdown,

137
00:05:09,286 --> 00:05:12,293
after Nvidia changed how it reports revenue by

138
00:05:12,386 --> 00:05:12,908
region.

139
00:05:13,547 --> 00:05:14,638
Steve's question:

140
00:05:15,068 --> 00:05:17,134
a company that can tell when a driver

141
00:05:17,239 --> 00:05:19,445
leaks to a reviewer can't track its own

142
00:05:19,491 --> 00:05:20,107
chips?

143
00:05:21,163 --> 00:05:23,392
One more thing doesn't add up for Zitron.

144
00:05:23,880 --> 00:05:27,328
In August, Nvidia promised 70% growth next year.

145
00:05:28,001 --> 00:05:30,462
Yet the Journal reported Jensen worried about a

146
00:05:30,544 --> 00:05:32,587
glut of chip-making capacity.

147
00:05:33,156 --> 00:05:34,584
You can't easily have both.

148
00:05:35,014 --> 00:05:36,151
The board, he says,

149
00:05:36,291 --> 00:05:38,833
has made clear Jensen isn't coming back.

150
00:05:39,507 --> 00:05:41,353
So can anyone do what he did?

151
00:05:41,433 --> 00:05:42,164
Part four.

152
00:05:42,931 --> 00:05:44,057
Nobody can replace him.

153
00:05:44,359 --> 00:05:45,636
The reassurance goes:

154
00:05:45,996 --> 00:05:47,435
whatever Nvidia loses,

155
00:05:47,505 --> 00:05:49,142
AMD and Broadcom pick up.

156
00:05:49,804 --> 00:05:52,033
Zitron says that misses what Jensen did.

157
00:05:52,404 --> 00:05:53,414
Start with cash.

158
00:05:54,192 --> 00:05:54,935
Over the past year,

159
00:05:54,970 --> 00:05:58,732
he says, Apple generated around $136 billion of

160
00:05:58,767 --> 00:05:59,580
free cash flow.

161
00:05:59,951 --> 00:06:02,343
Nvidia, about $127 billion.

162
00:06:03,086 --> 00:06:05,234
Microsoft, around $76 billion.

163
00:06:05,652 --> 00:06:06,813
And Broadcom?

164
00:06:07,230 --> 00:06:08,682
About $39 billion.

165
00:06:09,552 --> 00:06:11,271
Roughly a quarter of Nvidia's.

166
00:06:11,689 --> 00:06:12,931
Broadcom, he says,

167
00:06:12,966 --> 00:06:16,333
already broke what he calls Jensen's golden rule:

168
00:06:16,797 --> 00:06:18,620
never hold your customers' bags.

169
00:06:18,968 --> 00:06:22,242
He says Broadcom is backstopping around $60 billion

170
00:06:22,404 --> 00:06:24,425
of debt to lease chips to Anthropic.

171
00:06:24,854 --> 00:06:26,108
Jensen, in his view,

172
00:06:26,201 --> 00:06:27,048
never would have.

173
00:06:27,954 --> 00:06:30,160
Rivals tried copying his deals,

174
00:06:30,253 --> 00:06:32,192
but only Jensen had the balance sheet.

175
00:06:32,761 --> 00:06:34,467
Then there were the giant headlines.

176
00:06:34,896 --> 00:06:36,348
The Journal now reports,

177
00:06:36,371 --> 00:06:39,819
he says, that the firms behind a half-trillion

178
00:06:39,865 --> 00:06:40,992
dollar AI fund

179
00:06:41,351 --> 00:06:43,279
aren't committed to raising any money,

180
00:06:43,592 --> 00:06:45,136
and the number isn't meaningful.

181
00:06:45,624 --> 00:06:48,155
Then Nvidia's $100 billion plan

182
00:06:48,584 --> 00:06:51,685
with OpenAI, which Jensen later called never a

183
00:06:51,754 --> 00:06:52,428
commitment.

184
00:06:52,788 --> 00:06:55,458
And Nvidia kept writing smaller checks to keep

185
00:06:55,528 --> 00:06:56,631
buyers alive.

186
00:06:57,199 --> 00:06:59,800
Perplexity, the AI search startup,

187
00:07:00,032 --> 00:07:01,646
gets around $3 billion,

188
00:07:01,727 --> 00:07:04,374
he says, because it buys so much compute.

189
00:07:04,862 --> 00:07:08,369
His conclusion: this industry ran on Jensen's word

190
00:07:08,415 --> 00:07:09,936
and Nvidia's balance sheet.

191
00:07:10,366 --> 00:07:13,222
He points to reports that Jensen told customers

192
00:07:13,419 --> 00:07:16,113
to collect prepayments from their own customers.

193
00:07:16,542 --> 00:07:19,096
To Zitron, a sign the money was running

194
00:07:19,166 --> 00:07:19,561
out.

195
00:07:20,048 --> 00:07:22,115
If this helps you follow the AI money,

196
00:07:22,196 --> 00:07:24,274
subscribe to catch the next case file.

197
00:07:24,762 --> 00:07:26,468
Because without that backstop,

198
00:07:26,492 --> 00:07:29,081
some companies are suddenly very exposed.

199
00:07:29,133 --> 00:07:30,735
Part five. The death watch.

200
00:07:31,165 --> 00:07:32,488
So who's most exposed?

201
00:07:33,266 --> 00:07:35,994
Zitron points at the so-called neoclouds.

202
00:07:36,064 --> 00:07:36,923
They borrow money,

203
00:07:37,016 --> 00:07:38,340
buy Nvidia chips,

204
00:07:38,502 --> 00:07:40,104
use those chips as collateral,

205
00:07:40,302 --> 00:07:42,183
then rent the computing power to a few

206
00:07:42,217 --> 00:07:45,468
AI companies, often ones Nvidia invested in.

207
00:07:45,897 --> 00:07:47,987
Debt, chips, collateral,

208
00:07:48,219 --> 00:07:50,216
customers, and back to debt.

209
00:07:50,774 --> 00:07:52,387
He's put two of them on what he

210
00:07:52,480 --> 00:07:53,885
calls a death watch.

211
00:07:54,117 --> 00:07:57,171
Nscale, which he says needs more money than

212
00:07:57,217 --> 00:08:00,677
expected, and is still negotiating backing for a

213
00:08:00,758 --> 00:08:02,964
$45 billion data center

214
00:08:03,324 --> 00:08:05,135
for Anthropic in West Virginia.

215
00:08:05,495 --> 00:08:09,164
And Firmus, an Australian data center company that

216
00:08:09,233 --> 00:08:10,464
cut its IPO,

217
00:08:10,627 --> 00:08:11,404
then pulled it.

218
00:08:11,973 --> 00:08:13,808
Lambda, another chip renter,

219
00:08:13,970 --> 00:08:15,363
hopes to list next year.

220
00:08:16,269 --> 00:08:17,987
Why does Jensen matter here?

221
00:08:18,347 --> 00:08:20,065
Banks lent to these companies,

222
00:08:20,089 --> 00:08:23,072
he argues, believing Nvidia would step in if

223
00:08:23,142 --> 00:08:24,222
anything went wrong.

224
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:26,729
Now banks ask one question:

225
00:08:27,043 --> 00:08:28,738
is Nvidia backing this?

226
00:08:29,307 --> 00:08:30,816
If the answer is still waiting,

227
00:08:30,886 --> 00:08:32,012
the money doesn't come.

228
00:08:32,442 --> 00:08:33,672
It only takes one,

229
00:08:33,707 --> 00:08:34,392
he says.

230
00:08:34,880 --> 00:08:37,968
One insolvency, and lenders ask whether the whole

231
00:08:38,003 --> 00:08:39,930
sector is debt stacked on the same chips

232
00:08:39,953 --> 00:08:40,754
and customers.

233
00:08:41,184 --> 00:08:43,947
A tower where every floor rests on one

234
00:08:44,017 --> 00:08:44,528
beam.

235
00:08:45,201 --> 00:08:47,117
Steve offers a fair counterpoint.

236
00:08:48,022 --> 00:08:49,787
Blaming one man isn't quite right,

237
00:08:49,822 --> 00:08:50,531
he says.

238
00:08:50,890 --> 00:08:52,887
They were all part of this together.

239
00:08:53,317 --> 00:08:54,954
So what does all this mean for people

240
00:08:54,977 --> 00:08:56,858
who never bought a single share?

241
00:08:56,933 --> 00:08:57,873
Part six.

242
00:08:58,233 --> 00:08:59,174
Who pays?

243
00:08:59,534 --> 00:09:00,671
Start with gamers.

244
00:09:01,031 --> 00:09:03,690
Steve doesn't expect graphics card prices to fall

245
00:09:03,760 --> 00:09:04,723
any time soon.

246
00:09:05,153 --> 00:09:07,185
And don't wait for cheap used parts,

247
00:09:07,208 --> 00:09:07,870
he says:

248
00:09:08,357 --> 00:09:10,493
these data center chips were never built for

249
00:09:10,563 --> 00:09:11,713
ordinary computers.

250
00:09:12,142 --> 00:09:13,082
Then towns.

251
00:09:13,512 --> 00:09:16,159
Steve describes a data center his team visited.

252
00:09:16,577 --> 00:09:19,328
$77 million in tax breaks,

253
00:09:19,561 --> 00:09:21,267
in a relatively poor area.

254
00:09:22,010 --> 00:09:23,717
Fewer than 20 employees.

255
00:09:24,123 --> 00:09:25,540
Local hires, he says,

256
00:09:25,737 --> 00:09:27,351
mostly work in security,

257
00:09:27,572 --> 00:09:28,953
landscaping and cleaning.

258
00:09:29,615 --> 00:09:31,228
Once construction ends,

259
00:09:31,333 --> 00:09:34,421
Zitron adds, many sites need only 50 or

260
00:09:34,491 --> 00:09:35,338
60 people.

261
00:09:35,826 --> 00:09:37,835
And the planned West Virginia site,

262
00:09:37,916 --> 00:09:41,004
Steve says, may clear one of America's last

263
00:09:41,074 --> 00:09:42,653
chestnut-producing forests.

264
00:09:43,326 --> 00:09:45,137
The fallout lands on the people it always

265
00:09:45,184 --> 00:09:45,869
lands on.

266
00:09:46,229 --> 00:09:47,297
Not the billionaires.

267
00:09:47,784 --> 00:09:48,899
Then the economy.

268
00:09:49,096 --> 00:09:51,604
Zitron argues the industry's promise,

269
00:09:52,022 --> 00:09:54,298
that buying chips grows the economy,

270
00:09:54,600 --> 00:09:55,726
was oversold.

271
00:09:56,283 --> 00:09:58,036
Steve's version of the slogan:

272
00:09:58,233 --> 00:10:00,474
GPUs equal GDP.

273
00:10:00,892 --> 00:10:04,387
An economist told Zitron that official statistics may

274
00:10:04,433 --> 00:10:08,485
have overstated software's contribution to growth since 2022.

275
00:10:08,845 --> 00:10:09,971
That's his argument,

276
00:10:10,134 --> 00:10:11,237
not a settled finding.

277
00:10:12,014 --> 00:10:13,419
So here's your watch list.

278
00:10:13,733 --> 00:10:16,693
Does Nvidia file its quarterly report by December

279
00:10:16,786 --> 00:10:17,343
4th?

